			<p>Rabbi Zalman Schachter led a wedding of Jewish hippies, &#8220;Michelle and Richie&#8221; out in Deadwood, Oregon on a farm in the mid seventies which was very memorable. It was a Native American/Jewish themed wedding. The couple who were living in a teepee, had followed the traditional purification practices and micvah baths he required, for weeks before the marriage. We had a women&#8217;s sweatlodge, and a men&#8217;s sweatlodge the night before.<br />
     When all the many guests were seated in crescent rows of chairs in a field, with an aisle down the middle, Reb Zalman came out and asked the guests, &#8220;Why are we here?&#8221;<br />
       &#8220;Are we here to passively watch a show? To express love to our friends? To drink champagne and dance to celebrate a marriage?&#8221;<br />
       He paused and then said, &#8220;We are here to &#8220;witness&#8221; a ceremony. To &#8220;witness&#8221; something requires active participation from us as a group. We must have our &#8220;wits&#8221; about us, and be fully present. By our witnessing of the vows made by this couple, we become the container for these vows, the &#8220;glue&#8221; to cement, strengthen and support their desire to be united in marriage. So we must prepare ourselves before we are ready to actively perform this function.<br />
